course: dictionary
lesson: 11304
title: Yay: a cheer and a size
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Yay is a cheer. It shows joy, excitement or approval, like the word hooray. «Yay, it's snowing!» tells you the speaker is happy about the snow, even without any other clue.
explain: Yay is informal, so it belongs in chatty speech, messages and stories with real talk. In a formal letter or an essay, choose a plainer word such as wonderful.
explain: In American English, yay can also mean about this much or about this size, shown with the hands: «The fish was yay big» means it was about this long. Read the words around it to check which meaning fits.
explain: Sarcasm flips the meaning. «Oh yay, more homework.» is said in a flat voice and means the speaker is not pleased at all. Look at the tone and the other clues before you decide.
fact: Yay is an interjection, a word that shows a strong feeling on its own.
fact: «Yay» means a cry of joy, excitement or approval, like «hooray».
fact: In American informal English, «yay big» means about this big, shown with your hands.
fact: Sarcasm means saying the opposite of what you mean, often in a flat voice. «Oh yay» can mean you are not pleased at all.
fact: The French word «un cahier» ("ka-YAY") is an exercise book, where the h is silent. It sounds a bit like yay but means something different.
exercise: «Yay, it's snowing!» What does yay show here? | Sadness about the cold | Anger at the weather | *Joy and excitement | Boredom
exercise: «Oh yay, more homework.» The voice is flat. What does the speaker really feel? | Hungry for lunch | Very tired of the snow | Thrilled to bits | *Not pleased at all
exercise: The fish was «yay big». What does yay big mean? | Lost somewhere at sea | *About this big, shown with the hands | Gold and shiny | Very small, like a shrimp
test: In a story, a child shouts «Yay!» when the bell rings for the holidays. What does this show? | Tiredness after a long day | *Joy and excitement | Fear of school
test: Which sentence is best for a formal letter to the head teacher? | *The trip has been confirmed for Friday. | Yay, the trip is on! | Yippee, we get a day off!
exam: In American English, what does «yay high» mean? | Very low, like a worm | A kind of fish | *About this high, shown with the hands | Happy in a loud way
